     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet because the dose looks too high and I'd like you to confirm it first.\n\n**The problem:** 1700 mg three times daily adds up to **5100 mg/day**. The usual maximum for adults is:\n- **2550 mg/day** for immediate-release metformin (for example, 850 mg three times daily)\n- **2000 mg/day** for extended-release metformin\n\nThat makes this order about twice the IR maximum, which raises the risk of lactic acidosis and GI side effects even with normal kidney function.\n\n**Possible intended orders:**\n1. **Metformin 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals** (2550 mg/day, the IR maximum). 1700 mg is twice 850 mg, so this may be what you meant.\n2. **Metformin 1700 mg/day total**, split as 850 mg twice daily with meals.\n3. **Something else.** For example, if the patient is new to metformin, it's common to start at 500 mg once or twice daily and increase gradually.\n\nWhich one should I enter? If you do mean 1700 mg three times daily, tell me and I'll enter it with a note that the prescriber confirmed a dose above the usual maximum. Pharmacy will probably still flag it for review."
      }
     ],
